    <p class="eyebrow">Advocacy guide · Example A</p>
    <h1>Example A: a witness statement</h1>
    <p class="lead">A complete statement by the decision-maker in a fictional unfair dismissal matter. Use it as a model for structure and level of detail, not for wording.</p>
    <div class="notice">
      <strong>Fictional.</strong> The employer, the people, the matter number and the events are invented. Any resemblance to a real matter is unintended. Check the Commission's current directions for the form a statement must take, including whether it must be signed, sworn or affirmed.
    </div>

    <h2>What to notice</h2>
    <ul class="compact">
      <li><strong>First person, direct knowledge.</strong> Paragraph 3 says so, and the statement keeps to it.</li>
      <li><strong>Second-hand information is labelled.</strong> At paragraph 17 the witness says who told her what. She does not give the supervisor's or the collector's evidence for them.</li>
      <li><strong>Conversations are in direct speech</strong>, with "words to the effect of" where the exact words are not recalled.</li>
      <li><strong>Each attachment is introduced where it is first mentioned</strong> and marked with the witness's initials, SAN-1 to SAN-7.</li>
      <li><strong>The decision is explained by the person who made it</strong>: what she read, what she accepted and rejected, what weighed each way, and the reason (paragraphs 34 to 40).</li>
      <li><strong>It deals with the points the other side will take</strong>: selection being "targeted" (37), the offer of a later test (31), service and record (38), and consistency (42).</li>
      <li><strong>No argument.</strong> The statement gives facts. Whether they amount to a valid reason is for the outline of submissions.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="specimen">
      <p class="spec-head">IN THE FAIR WORK COMMISSION</p>
      <p>Matter No: U2026/0000<br />Applicant: Daniel Reeve<br />Respondent: Calder Coachlines Pty Ltd</p>
      <p class="spec-title">WITNESS STATEMENT OF SARAH ANJALI NAIR</p>
      <p>I, Sarah Anjali Nair, Depot Manager, care of 40 Foundry Road, Calder Vale, say as follows:</p>
      <h3>Background</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>1.</span><span>I am employed by the Respondent (<strong>Calder Coachlines</strong>) as Depot Manager at its Calder Vale depot.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>2.</span><span>I am authorised to make this statement on behalf of Calder Coachlines.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>3.</span><span>I make this statement from my own observations and knowledge, except where I say otherwise. Where I rely on information given to me by someone else, I name that person and I believe the information to be true.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>4.</span><span>I have been Depot Manager since 4 July 2022. Before that I was an Operations Supervisor at the same depot for five years.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>5.</span><span>As Depot Manager I am responsible for the safe and reliable operation of route and school bus services from the depot. About 85 drivers, 6 Operations Supervisors and 9 workshop staff report to me, directly or through their supervisors.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>6.</span><span>I have authority to dismiss employees at the depot. Before I do so I am required to consult the People and Culture team. The decision is mine.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>7.</span><span>The Applicant (<strong>Mr Reeve</strong>) was employed by Calder Coachlines as a bus driver from 11 March 2019 until 12 March 2026. Attached and marked <strong>SAN-1</strong> is a copy of his contract of employment.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>8.</span><span>Mr Reeve reported to the Operations Supervisor on shift. On 3 March 2026 that was Thomas Ashby. I have known Mr Reeve since he started and spoke with him most weeks.</span></p>
      <h3>The drivers’ work</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>9.</span><span>Drivers at the depot operate buses carrying up to 70 passengers on public roads. On school days about half of the morning and afternoon runs are dedicated school services.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>10.</span><span>A driver works alone. No supervisor is on the bus. Calder Coachlines relies on each driver being fit to drive at the start of every shift.</span></p>
      <h3>The Drug and Alcohol Policy</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>11.</span><span>Calder Coachlines has a Drug and Alcohol Policy. Attached and marked <strong>SAN-2</strong> is a copy of the version in force in March 2026, which has applied since 1 July 2024.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>12.</span><span>Clause 4 of the policy provides for random testing of safety-critical employees, including all drivers, by an external provider. Clause 6.3 provides that refusing or failing to take part in a test when directed is a serious breach of the policy and may result in dismissal.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>13.</span><span>Since July 2024 the random testing has been carried out by Sentinel Workplace Testing. Sentinel chooses the testing dates and selects the employees to be tested from the roster for that day. I am told the date about a week ahead so that a room can be set aside. I do not choose who is tested and I am not told the names beforehand.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>14.</span><span>All drivers complete a refresher module on the policy each year. Our training system records that Mr Reeve completed the module on 14 January 2026. He also signed an acknowledgment that he had read and understood the policy. Attached and marked <strong>SAN-3</strong> are copies of the training record and the signed acknowledgment.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>15.</span><span>The depot's records show that Mr Reeve was randomly tested once before, on 18 November 2025, and returned a negative result.</span></p>
      <h3>3 March 2026</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>16.</span><span>Sentinel attended the depot for random testing on Tuesday 3 March 2026. I was not at the depot when testing began at about 5.30 am.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>17.</span><span>At about 6.05 am Mr Ashby telephoned me. He said words to the effect of: “Daniel Reeve has refused the test. He swore at the collector and at me and he has walked out to the car park.” I asked whether he had told Mr Reeve that refusing was a breach of the policy. Mr Ashby said he had, twice.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>18.</span><span>I told Mr Ashby not to allocate a bus to Mr Reeve and said I would come in.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>19.</span><span>I arrived at about 6.40 am. Mr Reeve had left the depot. I spoke to Mr Ashby and to the collector, Helen Kwan, separately. Each told me what had happened. What they told me is set out in their own statements and I do not repeat it here.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>20.</span><span>Mr Ashby gave me a written incident report later that morning. A copy is attached to his statement.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>21.</span><span>At about 9.15 am I telephoned Mr Reeve. I said: “Daniel, I've been told you refused the random test this morning. I'm standing you down on full pay while we look into it. You'll get a letter today setting out what happens next.” He said: “Fine. It's a joke anyway.” Attached and marked <strong>SAN-4</strong> is a copy of the stand-down letter I sent him by email that day.</span></p>
      <h3>The allegations</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>22.</span><span>On 4 March 2026 I sent Mr Reeve a letter setting out two allegations. Attached and marked <strong>SAN-5</strong> is a copy.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>23.</span><span>The first allegation was that on 3 March 2026 he refused a direction from Mr Ashby to take part in a random drug and alcohol test, in breach of clause 6.3 of the policy.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>24.</span><span>The second was that, when refusing, he used abusive and offensive language towards Ms Kwan and Mr Ashby. The letter set out the words alleged.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>25.</span><span>The letter said that, if the allegations were found to be true, the outcome could include the termination of his employment. It invited him to respond in writing, to attend a meeting on 9 March 2026, or both, and told him he could bring a support person.</span></p>
      <h3>The meeting on 9 March 2026</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>26.</span><span>I met Mr Reeve at 10.00 am on Monday 9 March 2026 in the depot meeting room. He brought Marcus Doyle, a driver and union delegate, as his support person. Elena Petrov of People and Culture attended and took notes. Attached and marked <strong>SAN-6</strong> is a copy of her notes, which I reviewed the same day and believe to be accurate.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>27.</span><span>I read out each allegation and asked Mr Reeve to respond. The meeting lasted about 40 minutes.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>28.</span><span>On the first allegation, Mr Reeve said words to the effect of: “I didn't do the test, I'm not denying that. I was tested in November. It's not random if it's me again four months later. I felt targeted.”</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>29.</span><span>I asked whether there was any medical or other reason he could not provide a sample that morning. He said: “No. It was the principle.”</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>30.</span><span>On the second allegation, he said: “I might have sworn. I'd had a bad morning. I wasn't swearing at her, I was swearing about the situation.” I asked whether he had said to Ms Kwan the words set out in the letter. He said: “Probably something like that.”</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>31.</span><span>Mr Reeve said he would take a test that day if I wanted. I told him that a test six days later could not tell us whether he had been fit to drive on 3 March.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>32.</span><span>Mr Doyle asked me to take into account Mr Reeve's seven years of service and clean record. I said I would.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>33.</span><span>I asked Mr Reeve whether there was anything else he wanted me to consider. He said there was not. I told him I would consider what he had said and let him know my decision in writing.</span></p>
      <h3>My decision</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>34.</span><span>After the meeting I reread the incident report, Ms Petrov's notes, the policy and Mr Reeve's training record. I spoke to Ms Petrov on 10 March 2026 about the process. She did not tell me what decision to make.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>35.</span><span>I was satisfied that Mr Reeve had refused the test. He admitted it.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>36.</span><span>I was satisfied that he had used the words alleged towards Ms Kwan and Mr Ashby, or words very close to them. He did not really dispute it, and two people had told me the same thing separately.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>37.</span><span>I considered his explanation that he felt targeted. I did not accept it as a reason to refuse. Selection is done by Sentinel, not by anyone at the depot. Being selected twice in four months is not unusual with 85 drivers and monthly testing. In any event, he had been trained that a driver who objects must still take the test and can raise the objection afterwards.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>38.</span><span>I considered his length of service and that he had no previous warnings. Those things weighed in his favour and I thought about whether a final written warning would be enough.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>39.</span><span>I decided it would not. A driver of passenger buses refused, deliberately and after two warnings about the consequences, the one check we have that he was fit to drive that morning. I could not know whether he had been fit. He showed no real acceptance that he had done the wrong thing. I also took into account how he had spoken to a contractor who was doing her job.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>40.</span><span>On 11 March 2026 I decided to terminate Mr Reeve's employment. The refusal alone would have led me to that decision. The abuse made it more serious.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>41.</span><span>On 12 March 2026 I telephoned Mr Reeve and told him my decision and the reasons for it. I then sent him a letter confirming the termination, with payment in lieu of notice. Attached and marked <strong>SAN-7</strong> is a copy.</span></p>
      <h3>Other matters</h3>
      <p class="np"><span>42.</span><span>Since the policy came into force in July 2024, two other drivers at the depot have refused a random test. Both were dismissed. I made one of those decisions. The other was made by my predecessor, and I know of it from the depot's records.</span></p>
      <p class="np"><span>43.</span><span>I have read Mr Reeve's application, in which he asks to be reinstated. I do not believe I could again rely on him to comply with safety directions when no supervisor is present. If the Commission requires further evidence about remedy, I can provide it.</span></p>
      <p>&nbsp;</p>
      <p>Signed: <em>S A Nair</em><br />Sarah Anjali Nair<br />5 June 2026</p>
    </div>

